<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Matthys</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–1534</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jan Matthijs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Matthys was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Anabaptist preacher, originally a baker from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, who proclaimed himself a prophet of the coming end of the world. In 1534 he led radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> in taking over the city of Münster, which he declared the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, expelling those who refused rebaptism. He was killed in a sortie against the besieging army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at Easter 1534 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">Jan of Leiden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-matthys.jpg" alt="Jan Matthys" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Jan_Matthijsz,_overleden_Munster_1535,_Inventarisnummer_NL-HlmNHA_53013986.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
